  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 PM CDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nThe big story in the weather world tonight is the landfall of\nHurricane Isaias on Florida's Gulf Coast. IR satellite imagery\nshows a ridge of high pressure displaced just northwest of the\novert tropical action in the Gulf. \n\nThe thermal ridge was west of the area today and we still\nmanaged record highs at Omaha (91) and Lincoln (92). Southerly\nsfc winds have begun to slacken a bit with diurnal decoupling.\nStill, winds of 5-10 kts should help hold temps in the 60s. VWPs\nat OAX and EAX have yet to show elevated wind speeds picking \nup, but model soundings suggest this will happen after midnight\nas a LLJ develops. Gusts of 40-50 knots will be found just \n1-2kft above the sfc.\n\nThe pressure gradient tightens on Saturday, helping to mix\nstronger winds to the sfc. Gusts of 30-40 kts are forecast for\nnortheast Nebraska, just shy of WIND ADVISORY criteria. The\nwinds should help mix drier air to the sfc leading to heightened\nfire danger - especially north of the Platte River where\nsatellites corroborate drier soils. Minimum RH values will fall\nas far as 25-35%. It's not quite RED FLAG WARNING time, but it\nisn't far off considering the drying fuels. Please be careful \nwith ignition sources. \n\nHigh temps will likely sneak into the lower-90s in at least the\nmetro areas and will flirt with records for a second consecutive\nday. Some standing records (LNK/OLU/TQE/OFK) were just set/tied \nin 2024. Temps remain mild overnight as another LLJ develops\njust off the sfc leaving winds down here out of the south at\n10-15 mph.",
